Iris Predictor
========================================================
author: Francesco Pipitone
date: June 24, 2026
transition: fade

Project goal
========================================================

- **Idea**: classify a flower from the built-in `iris` dataset in a simple and visual way.
- **Target user**: anyone who wants to understand a Shiny app without advanced knowledge.
- **Strength**: essential interface with integrated documentation.

Why it is useful:

- it shows a complete example of input, server logic, and reactive output;
- it uses a dataset already included in R;
- it is easy to deploy to shinyapps.io.

How the app works
========================================================

- The user enters 4 flower measurements.
- The user selects the number of neighbors `k`.
- The user clicks **Predict species**.
- The server applies the **k-NN** algorithm to the `iris` dataset.
- The app returns:
    - the **predicted species**;
    - an **estimated confidence**;
    - a **plot** showing the new point;
    - the **5 most similar cases**.

Embedded R code
========================================================

```{r}
summary(iris)
```

This document runs R code during rendering and shows a summary of the dataset used by the app.

Why the app meets the assignment requirements
========================================================

- **UI input**: sliders, radio buttons, checkbox, action button.
- **Operations in `server.R`**: standardization, distances, classification.
- **Reactive outputs**: text, table, plot.
- **Documentation**: included directly inside the app, in the *How to use* tab.
- **Deployment**: the project is ready for `rsconnect::deployApp()` on shinyapps.io.

Conclusion
========================================================

**Iris Predictor** is a simple, complete, and easy-to-evaluate project.

Final message:

- clear interface;
- understandable reactive logic;
- integrated documentation;
- reproducible HTML5 presentation.

Thank you!
